(Description)
Given a string containing just the characters '(' and ')', return the length of the longest valid (well-formed) parentheses substring.

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    public int longestValidParentheses(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int max = 0;

        stack.push(-1);

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);

            if (c == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            }
            else {
                stack.pop();

                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i);
                }
                else {
                    max = Math.max(max, i - stack.peek());
                }
            }
        }

        return max;
    }
}

--- Approach ---
At first, I tried to solve this problem throughout stack of characters '(' and ')'.
But, in this approach, it should check whether it is valid parenthese all of the steps, and it was unefficient.

Therefore, I converted the stack from character to integers storing the indicies of parentheses.
1. It push the integer, -1 at first as a boundary.
2. If it meets (, it push the current index, else at the character ), it alsways pop up one from the stack.
  Because boundary integer was set at first, when the one index was popped, if the stack is empty, that means the number of ) is more than (.
  It was the signal that current parentheses were unvalid, then it sets another boundary as the current index.
3. Otherwise if the stack is not empty, it means that the current parentheses are valid.
  At this case, it updates the max length of valid parentheses.

I learned that I could use the stack as a function tool, not only sotring data through converting its data type from characters into Integer, indicies.
